WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.810
The task keeps the library's allocation

2
00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.441
lookup and serialization

3
00:00:04.441 --> 00:00:07.139
and removes the step that fills the table

4
00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:09.900
for eight-bit and sixteen-bit fingerprints.

5
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.050
The agent writes the two populate functions.

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.246
It may also change the struct

7
00:00:15.246 --> 00:00:16.785
the hash and the lookup

8
00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:18.877
but the interface stays the same

9
00:00:18.938 --> 00:00:20.785
and the evaluator counts every

10
00:00:20.846 --> 00:00:22.200
byte the filter keeps.

11
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.493
Filters hold ten thousand

12
00:00:24.493 --> 00:00:27.128
one hundred thousand or a million keys

13
00:00:27.128 --> 00:00:29.566
from three families of key patterns

14
00:00:29.566 --> 00:00:30.620
at both widths.

15
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.370
Each answers four query streams.

16
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.230
With a development seed and a private one

17
00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.920
that makes one hundred forty-four workloads.

18
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.572
Five metrics are measured against

19
00:00:41.634 --> 00:00:43.455
the authors' library: memory

20
00:00:43.455 --> 00:00:45.464
construction time, lookup time

21
00:00:45.464 --> 00:00:47.033
the false-positive rate

22
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:48.540
and the space overhead.

23
00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.929
The reference's memory follows

24
00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:53.376
from its layout: one byte per slot

25
00:00:53.376 --> 00:00:54.803
two at sixteen bits

26
00:00:54.803 --> 00:00:56.570
plus a forty-byte struct.

27
00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.022
That averages six hundred sixteen

28
00:00:59.086 --> 00:01:00.360
point two kibibytes.

29
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.609
To pass, a run must be strictly better than

30
00:01:03.671 --> 00:01:05.160
the reference on memory

31
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.332
construction time and lookup time

32
00:01:07.332 --> 00:01:08.511
on every workload

33
00:01:08.511 --> 00:01:11.180
and not measurably worse on the other two.

34
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.844
Each metric scores the reference's

35
00:01:13.912 --> 00:01:15.750
value over the sum of both

36
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.247
a tie is worth fifty

37
00:01:17.315 --> 00:01:19.085
and twice as fast is worth

38
00:01:19.153 --> 00:01:20.650
sixty-six point seven.

39
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.609
For the means of Claude Opus 5.5's run

40
00:01:23.609 --> 00:01:26.218
the build gives sixty-seven point two

41
00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:28.291
lookup fifty-four point three

42
00:01:28.291 --> 00:01:29.495
and memory fifty

43
00:01:29.495 --> 00:01:31.435
about fifty-seven point two

44
00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:32.840
as on the task page.

45
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.799
Pause and predict.

46
00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:36.945
A run builds twice as fast

47
00:01:36.945 --> 00:01:39.167
looks up ten percent slower

48
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.700
and ties on memory.

49
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.604
What is its score on one workload

50
00:01:43.604 --> 00:01:44.850
and does it pass?

51
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.720
It scores fifty-four point eight.

52
00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.828
But it fails twice over

53
00:01:52.828 --> 00:01:54.659
it loses on lookup time

54
00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:57.150
and a tie on memory is not a win.

55
00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.630
Three of sixteen models passed.

56
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.454
Claude Opus 5.5 had the best score

57
00:02:03.454 --> 00:02:05.079
fifty-seven point two

58
00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:08.260
building in about half the reference's time.

59
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.625
Gemini 4 Argon, running in Antigravity

60
00:02:11.625 --> 00:02:14.408
passed with fifty-five point three

61
00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:17.810
and GPT-6 Astra with fifty-two point eight.

62
00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.982
Opus's hash takes the first slot and the

63
00:02:21.046 --> 00:02:23.379
fingerprint from one multiplication

64
00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:25.646
and its struct keeps fewer fields

65
00:02:25.646 --> 00:02:27.590
that is its small memory win.

66
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:28.866
For large sets

67
00:02:28.927 --> 00:02:30.964
it groups the keys by the segment

68
00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:32.260
of their first slot.

69
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.182
A segment is complete once

70
00:02:34.243 --> 00:02:35.755
its group has been added

71
00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:38.234
so peeling runs right behind the adding

72
00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.290
while the data is still in cache.

73
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.073
The task promises distinct keys

74
00:02:43.142 --> 00:02:45.000
but a robust build must not

75
00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:46.790
loop forever on a repeat.

76
00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.016
Two copies of a key share all three slots

77
00:02:50.016 --> 00:02:52.139
each slot counts at least two

78
00:02:52.139 --> 00:02:53.714
and their XOR cancels

79
00:02:53.714 --> 00:02:55.700
so they can never be peeled.

80
00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.825
In our model, a repeat stopped peeling at

81
00:02:58.893 --> 00:03:00.585
five or six of nine keys

82
00:03:00.585 --> 00:03:02.210
for each seed we tried.

83
00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.875
Sorting finds repeats but is slow

84
00:03:04.943 --> 00:03:07.123
so Opus sorts only when a failed

85
00:03:07.191 --> 00:03:09.098
attempt looks like a repeat

86
00:03:09.098 --> 00:03:10.800
or after a few failures.

87
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.060
With the reference's layout

88
00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.220
only the struct's bytes can be won.

89
00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.100
Kimi K3 and two other runs kept exactly

90
00:03:18.165 --> 00:03:19.658
the reference's memory

91
00:03:19.722 --> 00:03:21.864
so they won memory on zero of one

92
00:03:21.928 --> 00:03:23.810
hundred forty-four workloads.

93
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.080
A tie is not a win.

94
00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.408
GPT-6.1 Sol had the fastest

95
00:03:28.482 --> 00:03:29.874
lookups on average

96
00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:32.145
but did not beat the reference

97
00:03:32.218 --> 00:03:34.270
on one workload, and failed.

98
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:36.649
That ends the Theory lecture

99
00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:37.890
with three lessons.

100
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:39.810
Theory sets the size

101
00:03:39.884 --> 00:03:41.902
the floor is log two of one

102
00:03:41.977 --> 00:03:43.920
over epsilon bits per key.

103
00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.348
Peeling is simple and fragile

104
00:03:46.348 --> 00:03:48.495
it needs enough slots per key

105
00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:49.950
and no repeated key.

106
00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.432
And every workload must win

107
00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:55.401
the best run halved construction time

108
00:03:55.477 --> 00:03:57.913
and GPT-6.1 Sol failed by losing

109
00:03:57.989 --> 00:03:59.740
lookup on one workload.

110
00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.850
Next time: Lecture 6, bioinformatics.
